// The holodeck: a pool of bodies a board materializes for the game it shows and releases when the game ends or another
// takes the board. While a body stands it is an ordinary body of the world (a rigid piece a player lifts, sets down or
// knocks over, a card a rule poses), so the game is read from where it settles; when it is released it leaves the
// population, and its slot is free for whatever the world admits next. A module library: it hands its importers the
// pool's placements, state, looks and the rule pieces of its lifecycle, and emits no document. It knows no game: which
// body stands for which token, where it stands and when it is wanted are the game's (../games/chess-station.puck's
// chess, ../games/patience-station.puck's cards).
//
// A pool is a list of bodies, each `{ id, prototype, look, at }`: `id` names its placement and keys its cells in the
// pool's rows, `prototype` the creation its placement names, `look` the look it wears (the prototype's own look, or one
// of `holodeckLooks` when the game changes it) and `at` its spawn point (in its parent's frame when the pool has one).
// Each pool is named by a key of its own (`pool`), which keys its cells in the pool rows. A body is an inhabited
// placement admitted by its pool's cell of `holodeckShown` (WorldPlacementInhabit.Pool) and counted by its own cell of
// `holodeckLive` over a one-sample disc: the population stands it while both are 1 and retires it otherwise
// (WorldPopulation.ReconcileInhabitCounts), so a game shown or put away is one write however many bodies its pool holds,
// and `placement:<id>` names the body, or no body while it is released (WorldPlacementInhabit.StandsOneBody). A
// materialized body takes the population's highest free slot, so a pool never renumbers a body the world authored at a
// fixed count. A count pass retires every released body before it admits any asked for, and a body the full table
// cannot admit yet is admitted at the next pass that has room, so a board swapping one game's tokens for another's needs
// room for the larger game only.
//
//   import "../standard/holodeck"
//   placements { holodeckPool(bodies: pool, kit: "piece", pool: "pieces") }
//   state { world { holodeckState(bodies: pool, pools: ["pieces"]) } }
//   rule "game-shown" {                            // the game's showing: one rule, one write of the pool's cell
//     local shown = gameOnBoard == 1
//     when holodeckShown.pieces != shown
//     mode: Level
//     holodeckShow(pool: "pieces", shown: shown)
//   }
//   rule "game-place" {                            // the game's own pose, once a body stands
//     local posed = holodeckMark(pool: "pieces", code: cell[$each])
//     when holodeckStands(pool: "pieces") and holodeckAt[$each] != posed
//     forEach: cell
//     mode: Level
//     // A topology a board facet anchors is read in that placement's frame; any other topology needs `frame:`.
//     poseCell(key: `placement:$each`, topology: board, expression: cell[$each], offset [0, 0.05, 0])
//     holodeckAt[$each] = posed
//   }
//
// A game that releases one body while the rest stand (a captured piece, a pawn whose look changes) sweeps the pool's
// roster and passes `holodeckSet` whether the body under `$each` is wanted.
//
// The pool's rows (inside a module instance they are the instance's own, so a station's pool never touches another's):
//
//   holodeckShown  per pool, 1 while its game is shown and its bodies stand, 0 while they are released
//   holodeckSince  per pool, the tick its bodies exist from at its latest showing, which every pose's mark folds in
//                  (`holodeckMark`), so the game poses each body afresh once the pool stands again
//   holodeckLive   per body, 1 while the game wants the body (it stands while its pool does), 0 while it is released
//   holodeckAt     per body, the mark the game last posed it at (`holodeckMark` of the game's own non-negative code),
//                  or, below zero, a body to pose afresh (one the game asked for again, moved by rule or asks to tidy)
//                  from the tick its negation names: a deadline the rules' due lists wake at, never a clock read every
//                  tick
//
// The doors: a pool is shown or put away only through `holodeckShow`, and one body asked for or released only through
// `holodeckSet`. Each writes the tick from which its bodies exist beside its admitting cell, so no pose can address a
// body the population has not admitted (a pose waits on `holodeckStands`, which reads both deadlines) and no released
// body keeps a mark the next materialization would trust.

// The ticks from asking for a body to posing it: the population admits a body at the end of the tick its count rose, so
// a pose two ticks on addresses a body that exists.
let holodeckArrival = 2

// The body under `$each` stands and exists. Read inside a rule sweeping the pool's roster; `pool` is the pool's key (a
// literal, or the binding that spells it).
function holodeckStands(pool) = ((holodeckShown[pool] == 1) and
  (holodeckLive[$each] == 1) and
  ($tick >= maximum(holodeckSince[pool], 0 - holodeckAt[$each])))

// The mark a pose of the body under `$each` at the game's code `code` (non-negative, under 2^20) leaves in
// `holodeckAt`: the code with the pool's showing folded in, so a mark from an earlier showing never matches.
function holodeckMark(pool, code) = code + 1048576 * holodeckSince[pool]

// The pool's bodies, inside a `placements` block: each an inhabited placement admitted by the pool's cell of
// `holodeckShown` and counted by its own cell of `holodeckLive`, under `kit`, spawning at `at` (in `parent`'s frame when a
// parent is named).
template holodeckPool(bodies, kit, pool, parent = null) {
  for body in bodies {
    placement $"{body.id}" {
      prototype: body.prototype
      inhabit {
        count {
          row: holodeckLive
          key: body.id
        }
        pool {
          row: holodeckShown
          key: pool
        }
        distribution {
          fill {
            name: "additive"
            offset: 0
            step: 0.001
          }
          // One sample: the facet stands at most one body, so `placement:<id>` names it for a pose or a read.
          region: disc(radius: 0.01, sampleCount: 1)
        }
        kit: kit
        look: body.look
        source: "Idle"
      }
      if parent != null {
        parent: parent
      }
      position: body.at
    }
  }
}

// The pool rows, inside a `state.world` block: every body's cells, and each of `pools`' cells. `shown` is what every
// pool starts at: 0, a pool whose game shows it, or 1 for bodies a world stands always while the game's rules still read
// the pool's cells.
template holodeckState(bodies, pools, shown = 0) {
  table holodeckLive capacity(auto) {
    for body in bodies {
      [body.id] = 1
    }
  }
  table holodeckAt capacity(auto) {
    for body in bodies {
      [body.id] = -1
    }
  }
  table holodeckShown capacity(auto) {
    for pool in pools {
      [pool] = shown
    }
  }
  table holodeckSince capacity(auto) {
    for pool in pools {
      [pool] = 0
    }
  }
}

// Looks a game changes while its body stands (a pawn queened on the last rank): each a look whose creation is read from
// its cell of the world's Text row `holodeckLook`, keyed by the look's name, at `scale`. A world expands
// `holodeckLooks` where its `looks` list is written and `holodeckLookState` in its `state.world` block; a rule changes a
// body's look by writing the cell and asking for the body afresh (released one tick, asked for the next), so the
// presentation reads the new creation when the body is admitted again. `looks` is a list of `{ name, prototype }`, the
// prototype the look starts with.
function holodeckLooks(looks, scale = 1) = map(looks, look => {
  motion { cues [], gaitAmplitude: 0, replayFrames: false, secondsPerFrame: 0 }
  name: look.name
  scale: scale
  source: creation(prototypeId: $"state.holodeckLook.{look.name}")
})
template holodeckLookState(looks) {
  row {
    name: "holodeckLook"
    kind: Text
    capacity: length(looks)
    cells: map(looks, look => { key: look.name, value: look.prototype })
  }
}

// The pool's door, spliced into a rule that fires only while the pool's cell differs from `shown` (`when
// holodeckShown[pool] != shown`): it writes the cell, standing or releasing every body of the pool at once, and the tick
// from which they exist. The write of `shown` closes the rule's own gate, so the price sheet charges a pool at rest
// nothing (RuleResponse.ClosesItself).
rule template holodeckShow(pool, shown) {
  holodeckShown[pool] = shown
  holodeckSince[pool] = $tick + holodeckArrival
}

// One body's door, spliced into a rule that sweeps the pool's roster and fires only while the body's count differs from
// `live` (`when holodeckLive[$each] != live`): it writes the count, asking for or releasing the body under `$each`, and
// marks it to be posed once it exists. The write of `live` closes the rule's own gate.
rule template holodeckSet(live) {
  holodeckLive[$each] = live
  holodeckAt[$each] = 0 - ($tick + holodeckArrival)
}

// Marks the body under `$each` to be posed again at its cell: a body knocked off its square that the game puts back, or
// one lifted off the board.
rule template holodeckTidy() {
  holodeckAt[$each] = -1
}
